matt [051006]grieshop@oznet.ksu.edu[051006][051006]I have not dealt with the cartrige in question (Marlin 444) and am assuming that this is a lever gun with a "hammer". However, I have a Marlin 30-30 that has the same problem with my handloads. Also a buddy of mine had a Win 30-30 that also had this problem. I have heard that this is common for many  post 1960's winchester 30-30's (my buddy had both an older and a newer win and only the new one had a problem with his handloads). <BR><BR>What both he and I noted with our handloads was that our primers were seated FARTHER into the case than the factory loads. So what I think is going on is that the hammer is not contacting the pin with sufficient force to detonate the primer. Just out of curiosity, do your primers fire if you hit them a second time? Mine usually seem to, you might try this with a "primer only" load. <BR><BR>One solution would be to carefully seat the primers a little less deeply (a pain in the arse). My buddy actually opened his firing mechanism up and altered it so the hammer hits the pin with greater force (by slightly bending one of the components....sorry can't remember which), thereby providing a "permanent" solution. You could probably get a gunsmith to do this for you if you are uncomfortable mucking with the firing mechanism. <BR><BR>Good luck![051006]10-06-2005 18:02
